    <!-- What pushes on what, and why. -->
    <string name="fin_connections_link_prices_fed">When prices rise too fast, the Fed raises its rate to cool the economy down.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_fed_mortgage">A higher Fed rate pushes up mortgage, car-loan and credit-card rates within weeks.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_fed_jobs">When borrowing gets expensive, businesses slow hiring and put off expanding.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_fed_stocks">Higher rates make safe savings more attractive than stocks, and make companies' borrowing cost more — so stock prices tend to fall.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_fed_credit">Rate rises that come fast can strain borrowers who took out loans when money was cheap.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_bonds_mortgage">Mortgage rates track the government's 10-year borrowing cost, usually 1.5–2 points above it.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_mortgage_homes">Expensive loans mean fewer buyers can afford a house, which cools home prices.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_jobs_mood">People with secure jobs and raises feel better about money — and spend more.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_mood_prices">When everyone's out buying, businesses can raise prices. When shoppers pull back, prices cool.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_stocks_mood">When their 401(k)s are up, people feel richer and spend more.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_credit_jobs">When companies can't borrow, they cut costs — and that usually means jobs.</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_link_debt_bonds">The more the government borrows, the more it may have to pay lenders to keep buying its debt.</string>

    <string name="fin_connections_title">How it all connects</string>
    <string name="fin_connections_intro">Nothing in the economy moves on its own. Tap any part to see what it pushes on and what pushes on it. Colors show today's reading: green calm, amber worth watching, orange-red a warning.</string>
